Concept. The roadmap moves TMCC from the API protection it already runs on Salt to Salt’s AI security capabilities, in a controlled and measurable way. It is built around Salt’s two AI capabilities. The first is AI Security Posture Management (AI-SPM), which answers “what AI do we have and is it configured safely?” It discovers AI agents, MCP servers, LLM connections and the APIs behind them, from code (GitHub Connect), cloud (Cloud Connect for AWS), the external surface, and runtime traffic. It then checks them against posture policies aligned to TMCC’s AI Security Standards. The second is AI Detection and Response (AI-DR), which answers “is anyone abusing that AI right now?” It watches AI gateway and agent telemetry for prompt injection, jailbreaks and anomalous agent behaviour, and links a model-level attack to the MCP and API activity that follows. Both capabilities sit on Salt’s Agentic Security Graph, a single map of how agents, models, tools and APIs connect. That shared map lets posture findings and runtime detections reinforce each other.

How it works. Each capability is broken down into the specific features that enable it, and the features are switched on one at a time. Nothing is turned on across the whole estate at once. Every feature follows the same pilot loop: pick a small scope (one GitHub org, one AWS account, one AI use case), enable it in monitor-only mode, observe two to four weeks of data, tune out the noise, and roll out more widely only after agreed exit criteria are met. Posture comes before detection, because inventory gives detection the context it needs, and detection runs in alert-only mode before any blocking. The first six months cover readiness and the discovery and runtime pilots. Months six to twelve cover phased rollout across the organisation, SIEM/SOC integration, and enforcement through TMCC’s existing standards and exception process. This keeps risk and alert fatigue low, gives each team a clear checkpoint, and leaves TMCC owning an operating AI security capability at the end of the year, not just a switched-on tool.
